# Relationship Between `ApiSuccessResponse` and `ApiErrorResponse`

Both `ApiSuccessResponse<T>` and `ApiErrorResponse<T>` are **sibling subclasses** of the sealed class `ApiResponse<T>`, along with `ApiEmptyResponse<T>`. They form a sealed class hierarchy to represent different API response states.

## Class Hierarchy

```kotlin
sealed class ApiResponse<T>
├── ApiSuccessResponse<T>      // Successful HTTP response (2xx, with body)
├── ApiEmptyResponse<T>        // Successful HTTP response (204 No Content)
└── ApiErrorResponse<T>        // Failed HTTP response (4xx, 5xx) or network error
```

## Key Differences

| Aspect | ApiSuccessResponse<T> | ApiErrorResponse<T> |
|--------|----------------------|---------------------|
| **Data** | `body: T`, `links: Map<String, String>` | `errorMessage: String` |
| **Purpose** | Wraps successful response data with parsed link headers | Wraps error message from failed response or exception |
| **Type** | `data class` (immutable, value-based equality) | `data class` (immutable, value-based equality) |
| **Special Features** | Parses pagination links; provides lazy `nextPage: Int?` property | Simple error message storage |

## Factory Method Pattern

The sealed class provides **factory methods** in its companion object that determine which subclass to instantiate:

```kotlin
// From ApiResponse.kt companion object
fun <T> create(response: Response<T>): ApiResponse<T> {
    return if (response.isSuccessful) {
        // Returns ApiSuccessResponse or ApiEmptyResponse
        if (body == null || response.code() == 204) {
            ApiEmptyResponse()
        } else {
            ApiSuccessResponse(body = body, linkHeader = response.headers().get("link"))
        }
    } else {
        // Returns ApiErrorResponse
        ApiErrorResponse(errorMsg ?: "unknown error")
    }
}
```

## Usage Pattern

The sealed class enables **exhaustive when expressions** for handling all response states:

```kotlin
when (response) {
    is ApiSuccessResponse -> // handle successful data
    is ApiEmptyResponse -> // handle 204 No Content
    is ApiErrorResponse -> // handle error with message
}
```

This is used throughout the repository layer (e.g., `NetworkBoundResource.kt`, `RepoRepository.kt`) for type-safe HTTP response handling.

